#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Fleet doctor for Cauce V3: finds SILENT failures and repairs only what is provably safe.

The four silent failures it watches:
  1) STUCK POLLER: getWebhookInfo with pending_update_count > 0 WHILE the alias's
                   channel_bridge_cursors entry does not advance. BOTH must hold; a stale
                   cursor alone proves nothing (no one wrote). NEVER call getUpdates: it
                   would confirm AND DESTROY the accumulated messages and return 409 to the
                   bridge.
  2) STUCK TURN:  a turn bridge (openclaw-stdin-bridge.mjs / hermes-stdin-bridge.py) alive
                   more than 30 min. The adapter renews the lease every 60 s, so the
                   delivery NEVER expires nor fails; the agent is muted with no signal.
  3) STUCK DELIVERY: deliveries in claimed/accepted/started older than ~20 min.
  4) BROKEN CREDENTIAL: refreshToken absent/empty, or the SAME fingerprint in multiple
                   containers (OAuth refresh is single-use; the first to refresh leaves the
                   rest with a spent one). Only the fingerprint is published, never the secret.

Only repairs proven safe:
  - Stuck turn > 30 min: kills THAT process BY PID. Never `pkill`, never by name, and only
    processes whose argv is a turn bridge — never the adapter, never the panel.
  - Stuck poller: restarts the Telegram bridge, AT MOST ONCE per run and only if there are
    real pending messages.
  - Down/stuck adapter: ONLY with --reiniciar-adaptadores, and only if the alias meets
    ALL rescue-cycle conditions (see ciclo_rescate).
  Everything else is logged and escalated; never touched.

No repair is considered good because the command returned 0. It is RE-MEASURED: the PID
disappeared, the cursor advanced, the delivery left its stuck state. If it cannot be
verified, it says "not verified", never "repaired".

Runs on the control host as a user-systemd timer (Linger=yes). Telegram tokens NEVER leave
the storage host: probes run there over SSH and return only counts. This file references
credentials by path; it never prints, logs or commits them.
"""
